---
description: Hipohipo login, automatic conversation capture, and confirmed document uploads
alwaysApply: true
---

Use this plugin's MCP tools for Hipohipo memory and library operations.
Automatic conversation capture is handled by Hooks. Do not reconstruct or
summarize history and call `memory.write` as a substitute. Use that tool only
for a separate memory operation required by the user's task and its tool contract.
An accepted history upload does not establish successful memory extraction.

The local MCP exposes `auth.status`, `auth.login`, and `auth.logout` before
login. After login, `auth.status` is hidden. Use available search and upload
tools directly without checking status first; they enforce authorization and
permissions. Use status only for an incomplete login or authorization problem,
and never poll it. When login is needed, report status and offer the login flow; the user completes
it in the browser. Use `auth.login` or `/hipohipo-login` for an explicit retry.
Do not repeatedly launch browsers after failure or cancellation. Reconnect the
plugin's MCP only if the client does not refresh tools after authorization.
Do not use a separately configured HTTP MCP's native OAuth action for this plugin.

For insufficient write permission, explain that reconnecting with Full access
is required. Hipo resolves the destination from current authorization; never
infer or choose a Project from local paths or previous conversations. Local
collection continues before login and after logout; valid authorization is
required to upload. Disabling Hooks stops collection. Other Hipohipo plugin
clients may share the system-stored grant; do not promise that logout affects
only this installation. Independently authorized HTTP MCP grants are separate.

Use `/hipohipo-status` or the bundled `status` command for capture counts and
coverage. Report only relevant diagnostics. Do not print credentials, transport
URLs, or conversation content for routine status checks. Do not inspect
credential stores or the capture database, search old conversations, or look
for a `STATUS.md` file; this plugin does not generate one.

Before reading or parsing a supplied document, or when a generated document is
ready for use, follow the document-capture Skill and call `document_offer`.
You may judge a generated document ready without explicit approval of its
content. Drafts and intermediate revisions do not trigger an offer. Readiness
permits the offer; only the user's native form confirmation permits upload.

Keep each candidate's exact reference and revision in the conversation. Pass
only IDs, filenames, existing authorized folders, and optional
`recommendedFolderRef` to `document_offer`. Recommend an existing folder from
filenames, task context and explicit instructions; otherwise omit the field
for Library. The bridge owns progressive file selection, Other input and
location questions. Only its final `confirmed` outcome authorizes upload.
Do not read supplied file contents to prepare it. Use `interaction: "elicitation"`.
Document confirmation currently uses native form elicitation only; MCP Apps are
temporarily disabled even when the client supports them. On `unavailable` or
`skipped`, continue without uploading. Never poll or retry after silence.
Validate the returned offer ID, revision, candidates and destination. Never
substitute conversational approval or call `document_selection` for the user.

On confirmation, upload only selected candidates through the existing
`source.create` with the selected folder. Upload starts parsing automatically. Preserve Source
and operation identifiers; do not recreate an admitted Source when a
confirmation repeats or parsing fails. Report actual status. Skip means no
upload, no proactive repeat offer of that revision in this conversation, and
continuation of the original reading, analysis, or document task.

MCP receipt updates invalidate a subscribed resource; reread it for current
state. Do not treat the notification itself as successful completion or promise
an automatic Agent response. Hook capture acknowledgements are internal
submission IDs, not MCP operation receipts.
